>
  <p><strong>Expect 30 FPS at launch.</strong> Rockstar hasn't promised a 60 FPS performance mode on any console, PS5 Pro included. Here's what's actually been said, and what it means for your setup.</p>

  <h2>What Rockstar has said</h2>
  <p>Rockstar North's Rob Nelson reportedly told creators visiting the studio that "the consoles are running the game at 30 frames per second" during development. Asked about 60 FPS, he said the technical team would need to decide whether a higher-frame-rate option arrives at launch, later, or not at all. The quote reached the public second-hand, through a creator's account reported by Kotaku. Rockstar hasn't published a frame rate spec.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>History lesson:</strong> GTA V shipped at 30 FPS on PS3 and Xbox 360. It only got a 60 FPS performance mode years later, in the PS5 and Xbox Series X|S version.</p></div>

  <h2>PS5 Pro</h2>
  <p>Rockstar hasn't announced any PS5 Pro-specific features. The extra GPU power would most likely go into sharper image quality and steadier frame pacing rather than double the frame rate. Open-world games with dense simulation, which GTA 6 clearly is, tend to be limited by the CPU, and the Pro's CPU is close to the base PS5's. Leaks claiming a 60 FPS mode on Pro are unverified, so don't buy a Pro just for that.</p>

  <h2>Xbox Series S</h2>
  <p>Series S versions usually run at lower resolution than Series X, with the same frame-rate target. The bigger issue is <strong>storage</strong>: the 212 GB preload fills most of the Series S's ~364 GB usable space. See the <a href="/gta-6/preload/">preload guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>PS5 features</h2>
  <p>PlayStation's official page lists these for GTA 6 on PS5:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Haptic feedback</strong> that reacts to your choices and the environment</li>
    <li><strong>Adaptive triggers</strong> with dynamic resistance</li>
    <li><strong>Controller speaker</strong> sound effects</li>
    <li><strong>Tempest 3D Audio</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Fast SSD loading</strong> across Leonida</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The game isn't playable on PS4, and there's no Xbox One version.</p>

  <h2>Does 30 FPS matter?</h2>
  <p>For a story-driven open world, a stable 30 FPS with good motion blur plays better than an unstable 45. If you're used to 60 FPS shooters it'll feel slower for the first hour, then you'll adjust. Turning on your TV's game mode cuts input lag either way.</p>

